  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Response-Recovery-Resilience for Conflict-Affected Communities in Ethiopia Project (3R4CACE)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia faces one of the most complex human mobility environments in the world, with a range of social, economic, political, and climatic factors driving populations within and outside its borders. The International Organization for Migration (IOM)&#x2019;s Displacement Tracking Matrix (DTM) reports that an estimated 2.7 million people (excluding Tigray region) are displaced across Ethiopia as of September 2022. Ethiopia is facing the worst drought in 40 years with 24.1 million people in need, and the recent conflicts in the country have resulted in extensive destruction of property. The peace agreement signed between the Federal Government of Ethiopia and the Tigray People&apos;s Liberation Front, ending a two-year armed conflict, is paving the way for recovery in affected regions through improved livelihoods, rehabilitation of social and economic infrastructures, and the promotion of social cohesion and harmony amongst affected populations. 
 
Through the Response-Recovery-Resilience for Conflict Affected communities in Ethiopia project, IOM is utilizing DTM&apos;s Mobility Tracking component and draw on its technical expertise in transition and recovery to conduct assessments to increase the evidence base on needs, infrastructure and access to services in the affected woredas, as well as ascertain the level of support required to address immediate needs and support post-conflict recovery.</narrative>
  </description>
